{"name":"Orion Blue","hex":"#40525f","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Orion (constellation)","blocks":[{"text":"Orion is a prominent set of stars visible during winter in the northern celestial hemisphere. It is one of the 88 modern constellations; it was among the 48 constellations listed by the 2nd-century AD astronomer Ptolemy. It is named after a hunter in Greek mythology."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/Orion_IAU.svg/330px-Orion_IAU.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orion_(constellation)"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/Orion_3008_huge.jpg/500px-Orion_3008_huge.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/Orion_3008_huge.jpg/1280px-Orion_3008_huge.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/Orion_3008_huge.jpg/500px-Orion_3008_huge.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Click on to see large image.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOrion_3008_huge.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/OrionCC.jpg/330px-OrionCC.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/OrionCC.jpg/330px-OrionCC.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Orion indicated on a clear night","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOrionCC.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Sidney_Hall_-_Urania%27s_Mirror_-_Orion_%28best_currently_available_version_-_2014%29.jpg/500px-Sidney_Hall_-_Urania%27s_Mirror_-_Orion_%28best_currently_available_version_-_2014%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Sidney_Hall_-_Urania%27s_Mirror_-_Orion_%28best_currently_available_version_-_2014%29.jpg/500px-Sidney_Hall_-_Urania%27s_Mirror_-_Orion_%28best_currently_available_version_-_2014%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Orion as depicted in Urania's Mirror, a set of star chart cards published in London c. 1825","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASidney_Hall_-_Urania's_Mirror_-_Orion_(best_currently_available_version_-_2014).jpg"},{"srcset":"http